# tvOS Browser

A browser for Apple TV, built around `WKWebView` and controlled with the Siri Remote. It has tabs, a pointer, a TV-friendly start page and menu, favorites, history, page zoom, content blocking, a cursor magnifier, and video fullscreen support.

The app uses private tvOS/WebKit APIs. It is intended for personal development and sideloading, not App Store distribution. There is no prebuilt binary in this repository.

## Requirements and project

- macOS with Xcode and a tvOS Simulator or a paired Apple TV.
- tvOS deployment target: **15.6**.
- Xcode project: [`_Project/Browser.xcodeproj`](_Project/Browser.xcodeproj); scheme: `Browser`.
- Bundle identifier in the current project: `homeTvBrovser11051991`.
- The app is written primarily in Objective-C. Its browser engine is the system WebKit, so site and media compatibility also depend on the installed tvOS version.

Open the project in Xcode, select the `Browser` scheme and a tvOS destination, then Build/Run. Keep the project's existing automatic signing and development-team settings when building for a device.

### Build and run in a simulator

Find an available simulator ID with `xcrun simctl list devices available`, set `SIMULATOR_ID` to that value, then run from the repository root:

```sh
xcodebuild -project _Project/Browser.xcodeproj -scheme Browser -configuration Debug \
  -destination "platform=tvOS Simulator,id=$SIMULATOR_ID" \
  -derivedDataPath /private/tmp/tvosbrowser-simulator-build \
  CODE_SIGNING_ALLOWED=NO build

xcrun simctl install "$SIMULATOR_ID" \
  /private/tmp/tvosbrowser-simulator-build/Build/Products/Debug-appletvsimulator/Browser.app
xcrun simctl launch "$SIMULATOR_ID" homeTvBrovser11051991
```

The simulator can check layout and focus navigation. Pointer gestures and video behavior should also be checked on an Apple TV.

### Build and run on an Apple TV

Find the device IDs first. The ID shown by Xcode destinations and the CoreDevice identifier can differ. Set `XCODE_DEVICE_ID` and `COREDEVICE_ID` to the respective values:

```sh
xcrun devicectl list devices
xcodebuild -project _Project/Browser.xcodeproj -scheme Browser -showdestinations
```

Build a signed Debug app, then install and launch it:

```sh
xcodebuild -project _Project/Browser.xcodeproj -scheme Browser -configuration Debug \
  -destination "platform=tvOS,id=$XCODE_DEVICE_ID" \
  -derivedDataPath /private/tmp/tvosbrowser-device-build \
  -allowProvisioningUpdates build CODE_SIGN_STYLE=Automatic

xcrun devicectl device install app --device "$COREDEVICE_ID" \
  /private/tmp/tvosbrowser-device-build/Build/Products/Debug-appletvos/Browser.app
xcrun devicectl device process launch --device "$COREDEVICE_ID" homeTvBrovser11051991
```

A successful build or launch only confirms installation; verify the interaction on the TV itself. Do not install a new build on a TV that someone is using without coordinating with them.

## Siri Remote controls

| Input | On a web page | In other views |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Touch surface movement | Move the pointer | Navigate focus where applicable |
| Center click | Click under the pointer; when using directional focus on New Tab, activate the focused item | Select the focused control |
| Hold Center | Toggle the cursor magnifier | — |
| Up / Down | Smooth scroll; hold to scroll continuously with gradual acceleration | Move focus on New Tab and menus |
| Left / Right | Go back / forward within the active tab's WebKit history | Move focus on New Tab and menus |
| Double Left | Open the tab overview | — |
| Back / Menu | Open or close the browser menu; leave video fullscreen or turn off the magnifier when active | Close the current overlay; from the tab overview return to the previously active tab |
| Play/Pause | Control video playback | Activate a focused New Tab item; hold on a Favorite to edit it; close the focused tab in the tab overview |
| Double Up | — | Close the focused tab in the tab overview |

The pointer hides after inactivity and immediately when directional navigation starts. Moving on the touch surface brings it back. The magnifier follows the pointer, refreshes while stationary, and uses a slower pointer speed. At screen edges, the part of the lens outside the screen is clipped.

Back is an app navigation control, not a second way to traverse page history. Use Left and Right for page history. When a tab has no earlier or later WebKit entry, those buttons have nowhere to go; the New Tab page is not inserted into that history.

## Start page, tabs, and saved pages

The **New Tab** page contains an address/search field, Favorites, and the ten most visited URLs. Arrow keys navigate these controls; Play/Pause acts as Enter. **All History** opens the full visit list. Press Center on a visit to mark it; the checkmark is on the left. The fixed action row above the list lets you open one selected visit, delete selected visits, select all, or clear all history with confirmation without scrolling to the bottom. Play/Pause also opens history actions. Favorites can be opened, edited, and deleted; hold Play/Pause on a selected Favorite to edit it.

The tab overview has a large **+** button for New Tab. The empty New Tab page is transient: it does not occupy a saved tab slot, and the browser saves ordinary tabs and the active tab for restoration. Double Up or Play/Pause closes the focused ordinary tab. The old five-tab limit has been removed.

Visits and Favorites are stored in `BrowserHistory.sqlite` using SQLite. On tvOS the app prefers Application Support, then Documents, then Caches. It copies an existing database from Caches or Documents into Application Support on first launch after this change and retains the older preferences backup path. A simulator build replacement preserved all 25 existing visits during this migration; persistence after removing and reinstalling the app still needs separate verification.

## Browser menu

Press **Back/Menu** on a page to open the menu. The top toolbar contains **Home**, **Reload**, a compact address field showing the current domain, **New Tab**, and **Tabs**. Selecting the address field opens editing of the full URL. The tiled sections below it are:

| Section | Controls |
| --- | --- |
| Quick Actions | Zoom Out, Reset Zoom, Zoom In, Add Favorite, History |
| Settings | Ad Block, Magnifier, Fit to Screen, Full Screen Player, Mobile Site |
| Tools | Debug, User Guide, Clear Cache, Clear Cookies |

Zoom changes in 10% steps, from 50% to 200%. Reset Zoom returns to 100%. The browser applies both page zoom and text sizing; the current zoom preference is restored after reload. The zoom controls and settings toggles keep the menu open so changes can be checked immediately.

- **Ad Block** applies WebKit content-blocking rules. Its ON/OFF state reflects the app's setting; coverage depends on the rules and the site's ad delivery. Turning it off removes the rule list from the active WebView.
- **Magnifier** toggles the lens under the pointer. Hold Center is the shortcut.
- **Fit to Screen** changes WebKit's page-scaling behavior.
- **Full Screen Player** enables the browser's video fullscreen behavior, including an expanded video with a dimmed background when supported by the page.
- **Mobile Site** switches the user agent between desktop and mobile modes; sites can respond differently to each.
- **Debug** opens **Media Diagnostics** and **Inspect WebKit Media Prefs**. These are developer tools, not normal browsing actions.
- **User Guide** opens the in-app visual guide.

## Video support and limits

The app uses tvOS WebKit and AVPlayer rather than bundling a general-purpose decoder. A page can play video only when its source, container, codecs, delivery method, and any DRM work with the system playback stack. A fullscreen toggle cannot make an unsupported format playable. Some sites also require a user gesture or site-specific JavaScript behavior before their player starts.

The browser includes compatibility handling for embedded players, fullscreen presentation, and an expanded theater-style view above page content. It does not guarantee that every video, advertisement, or custom player will render correctly. If a video fails, record the page URL, the exact action, what appeared on screen, the tvOS version, and relevant WebKit/AVPlayer logs. The menu's Debug tools can help distinguish player configuration from a page interaction problem.

